"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a single exceptional value of\u202f\\(k\\); the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nIn other words, a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists **iff** the number of sunny lines is at least\u202f1 and at most\u202f\\(n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions  \n   \\(\\ell_{x}\\!:\\;x=\\text{const},\\;\\ell_{y}\\!:\\;y=\\text{const},\\;\\ell_{d}\\!:\\;x+y=\\text{const}\\).  \n   *A line is **sunny** otherwise.*\n\n2. **Lower bound \u2013 at least one sunny line is necessary.**  \n   Assume all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n   Each line belongs to one of the three families \\(\\mathcal F_{x},\\mathcal F_{y},\\mathcal F_{d}\\).  \n   By the pigeon\u2011hole principle one of the families contains at least \\(\\lceil n/3\\rceil\\ge 1\\) lines.  \n   A line of family \\(\\mathcal F_{x}\\) (resp. \\(\\mathcal F_{y}\\)) fixes the \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate (resp. the \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate) of every point it contains; a line of family \\(\\mathcal F_{d}\\) fixes the sum \\(x+y\\).  \n   The set of required points is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\) have distinct \\(x\\)\u2013coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)\u2013coordinates and distinct sums, so they cannot be covered simultaneously by a single line of any one family.  \n   Consequently at least two different families are needed, i.e. at least two non\u2011sunny directions must appear among the \\(n\\) lines.  \n   Hence a configuration with **zero** sunny lines is impossible.\n\n3. **Upper bound \u2013 at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines are possible.**  \n   Suppose exactly \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny. Then only one line is non\u2011sunny; without loss of generality it is of the form \\(x= c\\) (the other two cases are symmetric).  \n   The line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most one point of the set \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n-1)\\}\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so each of them meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in a distinct point.  \n   Hence at most \\(n-1\\) points with abscissa\u202f\\(1\\) can be covered, but the set \\(T_{n}\\) contains **\\(n-1\\)** such points.  \n   To cover all of them we would need the vertical line \\(x=1\\) itself, contradicting the assumption that there is only one non\u2011sunny line.  \n   Therefore a configuration with \\(n-1\\) (or \\(n\\)) sunny lines cannot exist, and we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 two non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Take the vertical line \\(L_{1}:x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(L_{2}:y=1\\).  \n   These two lines cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose abscissa or ordinate equals\u202f1.\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 the remaining points.*  \n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) the points of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfying \\(a+b=s\\) lie on the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{s}:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(D_{s}\\) is non\u2011sunny (it belongs to the family \\(\\mathcal F_{d}\\)).  \n   Choose a set  \n\n   \\[\n   S\\subseteq\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   of size \\(k\\).  \n   For every \\(s\\in S\\) we keep the line \\(D_{s}\\) (these will be the sunny lines after a slight rotation, see below).  \n   For the remaining values \\(s\\notin S\\) we replace \\(D_{s}\\) by a *sunny* line \\(U_{s}\\) that passes through **all** points of \\(T_{n}\\) with sum \\(s\\) and is not parallel to any forbidden direction.  \n   Such a line exists because any two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\) with the same sum \\(s\\) are not collinear with a direction parallel to the axes or to \\(x+y=0\\); a line through them therefore has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Moreover, for different values of \\(s\\) the lines \\(U_{s}\\) are distinct, because each \\(U_{s}\\) contains a point whose sum equals \\(s\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 counting.*  \n   We have used exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\;(\\text{the lines }L_{1},L_{2})\\;+\\;|S|\\;(\\text{non\u2011sunny }D_{s})\\;+\\;(n-2-|S|)\\;(\\text{sunny }U_{s})\\;=\\;n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.  \n   The number of sunny lines is precisely \\(n-2-|S| = k\\).  \n   Since \\(|S|\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), the parameter \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-2\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 verification.*  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) belongs either to \\(L_{1}\\) (if \\(a=1\\)), to \\(L_{2}\\) (if \\(b=1\\)), or to the line corresponding to its sum \\(s=a+b\\).  \n   Hence all points are covered, the lines are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary conditions derived in steps\u202f2\u20133 are also sufficient by the explicit construction of step\u202f4.  \n   Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions  \n\\(\\mathbf e_{x}=(1,0),\\;\\mathbf e_{y}=(0,1),\\;\\mathbf e_{d}= (1,-1)\\).  \nOtherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**; consequently a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{x}=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal F_{y}=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal F_{d}=\\{x+y=c\\},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least one sunny line is necessary\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nEach line belongs to one of the families (1).  \nIf all lines were taken from a single family, they would be parallel and could cover at most one point of \\(T_{n}\\) with a given abscissa (or ordinate, or sum), which is impossible because \\(|T_{n}|= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\).  \nHence at least two families appear among the \\(n\\) lines.\n\nSuppose only one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{x}\\) (say \\(x=c\\)) is present.  \nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) with abscissa \\(c\\) are covered, but the points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,n-1),\\qquad P_{2}=(n-1,1)\n\\]\n\nhave distinct abscissae, distinct ordinates and distinct sums.  \nNo line of \\(\\mathcal F_{y}\\) can contain both, and no line of \\(\\mathcal F_{d}\\) can contain either of them together with a point of the other family.  \nThus at least **two** non\u2011sunny directions are required, which forces the existence of at least one sunny line among the \\(n\\) lines.  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\nk\\ge 1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  No configuration with \\(n-1\\) (or \\(n\\)) sunny lines\n\nAssume that exactly one line is non\u2011sunny; without loss of generality it is vertical,\n\\(L:x=c\\).  \nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny, hence none of them is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \nEach sunny line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in a distinct point, because two different sunny lines cannot intersect the same vertical line at the same point (otherwise they would coincide).  \n\nThe set \\(T_{n}\\) contains the \\(n-1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n-1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTo cover all points in (3) we need at least \\(n-1\\) distinct lines intersecting the line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf the unique non\u2011sunny line were not \\(x=1\\), then the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would have to provide those intersections, which is possible only if one of them is exactly \\(x=1\\); but \\(x=1\\) is non\u2011sunny, contradicting the assumption that there is only one non\u2011sunny line.  \n\nHence a configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist, and a fortiori none with \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) satisfying (2)\u2013(4).  \nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nS\\subseteq\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad |S|=n-2-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDefine the following collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n* **Two non\u2011sunny lines**\n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose abscissa or ordinate equals\u202f1.\n\n* **Lines corresponding to the sums \\(s\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).**  \n\n  \u2013 If \\(s\\in S\\) we keep the line  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{s}:x+y=s ,\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  which belongs to the non\u2011sunny family \\(\\mathcal F_{d}\\).\n\n  \u2013 If \\(s\\notin S\\) we replace \\(D_{s}\\) by a **sunny** line \\(U_{s}\\) defined as follows.\n    Choose any two distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\) with sum \\(s\\); for instance  \n\n    \\[\n    P_{s}=(1,s-1),\\qquad Q_{s}=(s-1,1).\n    \\]\n\n    The line through \\(P_{s}\\) and \\(Q_{s}\\) has slope  \n\n    \\[\n    m_{s}= \\frac{1-(s-1)}{\\,s-1-1\\,}= -\\frac{s-2}{s-2}= -1,\n    \\]\n\n    which is **forbidden**.  \n    To obtain a sunny line we perturb one of the two points by adding \\(1\\) to its\n    abscissa (or ordinate).  For example, set  \n\n    \\[\n    \\widetilde P_{s}=(2,s-2),\\qquad \\widetilde Q_{s}=(s-1,1).\n    \\]\n\n    Since \\(s\\ge 3\\), both points belong to \\(T_{n}\\).  \n    The slope of the line \\(U_{s}\\) through \\(\\widetilde P_{s}\\) and \\(\\widetilde Q_{s}\\) is  \n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1-(s-2)}{\\,s-1-2\\,}= \\frac{3-s}{s-3},\n    \\]\n\n    which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(U_{s}\\) is sunny.\n    Moreover \\(U_{s}\\) contains the original point \\((s-1,1)\\) and therefore\n    covers **all** points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose sum equals \\(s\\) (the only such points are\n    \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\), both lie on \\(U_{s}\\)).\n\nAll lines listed in (6)\u2013(7) are distinct: the two vertical/horizontal lines are\ndifferent from each other and from any line of the form \\(x+y=c\\) or \\(U_{c}\\);\ndifferent values of \\(s\\) give different constants \\(c\\) and therefore different\nlines.\n\n* **Counting sunny lines.**  \n  The sunny lines are precisely the \\(U_{s}\\) with \\(s\\notin S\\); their number is  \n\n  \\[\n  |\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus S| = (n-2)-|S| = k .\n  \\]\n\nThus we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Sections\u202f1 and\u202f2 we have shown that any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n1\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nSection\u202f3 furnishes, for every integer \\(k\\) in this interval, an explicit construction\nrealising such a configuration.  Hence the condition is also sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore, for a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated requirements is exactly**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
