"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following two statements  \n\n* **Lemma\u202f1.**  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and contains **no** sunny line (i.e. \\(k=0\\) is always attainable).  \n\n* **Lemma\u202f2.**  If a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all the required points and contains **at most one** non\u2011sunny line, then necessarily \\(n\\le 2\\). Consequently, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) any admissible configuration must contain **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n  \\[\n  0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n  \\]\n\nThese lemmas give the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) and show that the extreme value \\(k=0\\) is always realizable. The remaining values \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) are not settled by the present work.\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*The overall strategy* is to separate the problem into two parts. First we exhibit an explicit construction that uses only non\u2011sunny lines, thereby proving that \\(k=0\\) is always possible. Second we prove a lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines that any covering must contain.  \n\n*Key Lemma\u202f1 (Construction with only non\u2011sunny lines).*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) consider the line  \n\\[\nL_t:\\;x+y=t .\n\\]\nAll these lines have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non\u2011sunny. The set \\(\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines. Every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_{a+b}\\); the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_2\\), the point \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lie on \\(L_3\\), etc. Adding the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which is also non\u2011sunny) we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that covers all required points. Hence \\(k=0\\) is feasible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n*Key Lemma\u202f2 (At least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary).*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering of the required points uses at most one non\u2011sunny line. There are three possible types of non\u2011sunny lines: horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)), or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n\n1. **If the unique non\u2011sunny line is horizontal**, say \\(y=c\\), then all points with \\(b\\neq c\\) must be covered by sunny lines. A sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it can intersect each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point. Since there are \\(n\\) distinct values of \\(b\\) among the required points (\\(b=1,2,\\dots ,n\\)), at least \\(n\\) sunny lines would be needed, contradicting the hypothesis that the total number of lines is \\(n\\).  \n\n2. **If the unique non\u2011sunny line is vertical**, the same argument applied after swapping the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) yields a contradiction.  \n\n3. **If the unique non\u2011sunny line is diagonal**, say \\(x+y=c\\), then every required point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\neq c\\) must lie on a sunny line. Two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) with the same sum \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\neq c\\) cannot lie on the same sunny line, because any line of slope different from \\(-1\\) meets the family of diagonals \\(\\{x+y=t\\}\\) in at most one point. The required set contains the \\(n-1\\) distinct diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); each of them (except possibly the one equal to \\(c\\)) would therefore require a distinct sunny line. Hence at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are needed, again contradicting the total of \\(n\\) lines.  \n\nIn all three cases we obtain a contradiction unless \\(n\\le 2\\). Since the problem assumes \\(n\\ge 3\\), any admissible configuration must contain **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Putting the lemmas together* we have proved that for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and that the endpoint \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\\[\nL_t:\\;x+y=t .\n\\]\nAll the lines \\(L_t\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore they are not sunny.  \nThe set \\(\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines.  \n\nConsider any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) satisfying the required conditions. Its sum \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\). If \\(a+b\\le n\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\). The only point not covered by the family \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) is \\((1,1)\\), whose sum equals \\(2\\) and is already on \\(L_2\\); the point \\((1,n)\\) (sum \\(n+1\\)) is not covered because we stopped at \\(t=n\\). To cover the remaining diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) we add the vertical line  \n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\nThe line \\(V\\) is also non\u2011sunny (its slope is infinite). Every point with \\(a=1\\) and \\(2\\le b\\le n\\) lies on \\(V\\); in particular all points of the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) have abscissa \\(1\\) and are therefore covered.  \n\nThus the family  \n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}=\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\cup\\{V\\}\n\\]\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every required point. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*Let \\(n\\ge 3\\). In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at least two of the lines must be non\u2011sunny. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) contains at most one non\u2011sunny line. There are three possible types for a non\u2011sunny line; we treat them separately.\n\n1. **The unique non\u2011sunny line is horizontal**, say \\(y=c\\) with some integer \\(c\\).  \n   Every required point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq c\\) must belong to a sunny line.  \n   A sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\); therefore it meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point.  \n   The set of required points contains exactly one point on each horizontal line \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) (for instance \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(y=b\\)). Hence we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines to cover all those points, contradicting \\(|\\mathcal{C}|=n\\).\n\n2. **The unique non\u2011sunny line is vertical**, say \\(x=c\\).  \n   By symmetry with the previous case (swap the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)) we obtain the same contradiction.\n\n3. **The unique non\u2011sunny line is diagonal of slope \\(-1\\)**, say \\(x+y=c\\).  \n   For any integer \\(t\\neq c\\) the diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) contains exactly one required point with each abscissa \\(a\\) ranging from \\(1\\) to \\(\\min(t-1,n+1-t)\\).  \n   A sunny line has slope different from \\(-1\\); consequently it intersects each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) in at most one point.  \n   The required set contains points on the \\(n-1\\) distinct diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\). All of them except possibly the one equal to \\(c\\) must be covered by sunny lines, and each such diagonal needs a distinct sunny line. Hence at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are required, again contradicting \\(|\\mathcal{C}|=n\\).\n\nIn every possible case the assumption that \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) contains at most one non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction when \\(n\\ge 3\\). Therefore any admissible family must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines. Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Consequences  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we know that the value \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that for \\(n\\ge 3\\) no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\); equivalently every admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThus we have established the following rigorous partial classification:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{If }n\\ge 3,\\text{ then any admissible }k\\text{ satisfies }0\\le k\\le n-2,\\text{ and }k=0\\text{ is realizable.}}\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) remains open. \u220e"
