"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete description of all admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is:\n\n* **Lemma\u202f1.**  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and contains **no** sunny line (i.e. \\(k=0\\) is always attainable).\n\n* **Lemma\u202f2.**  In any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers the required points, at least two of the lines must be non\u2011sunny. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n  \\[\n  0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n  \\]\n\nThus we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) and the extremal value \\(k=0\\) is realizable. The intermediate values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) remain unresolved.\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the family of diagonals \\(x+y=t\\) for \\(t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) (all have slope \\(-1\\), hence are non\u2011sunny) together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\). Every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies either on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\) (if \\(a+b\\le n\\)) or on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (if \\(a+b=n+1\\)). This yields \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines, proving Lemma\u202f1.\n\n2. **Lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Assume a covering uses at most one non\u2011sunny line. Three cases are possible for that line:\n\n   *Horizontal.*  If the unique non\u2011sunny line is \\(y=c\\), then every point with \\(b\\neq c\\) must lie on a sunny line. A sunny line (slope \\(\\neq0\\)) meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point, yet the set of required points contains \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points spread over the \\(n\\) distinct horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\). Hence at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\) sunny lines would be needed, contradicting the total of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Vertical.*  The same argument after swapping the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) yields a contradiction.\n\n   *Diagonal of slope \\(-1\\).*  If the unique non\u2011sunny line is \\(x+y=c\\), then each of the other diagonals \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(t\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{c\\}\\) must be covered by sunny lines. A sunny line (slope \\(\\neq-1\\)) intersects any diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) in at most one point, while each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) contains \\(t-1\\ge1\\) required points. Consequently each such diagonal needs a distinct sunny line, giving at least \\(n\\) sunny lines, again impossible for \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   In all three possibilities we obtain a contradiction, so any admissible configuration must contain **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines. Hence \\(k\\le n-2\\), establishing Lemma\u202f2.\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 we obtain the rigorous partial classification stated above.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\\[\nL_t:\\;x+y=t .\n\\]\nAll the lines \\(L_t\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore they are not sunny.  \nThe set \\(\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines.\n\nConsider any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) satisfying the required conditions. Its sum \\(s=a+b\\) is an integer with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(s\\le n\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(L_s\\).\n* If \\(s=n+1\\) then \\(a\\) can be any integer from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) and \\(b=n+1-a\\). All these points have abscissa \\(1\\); they are therefore covered by the vertical line  \n  \\[\n  V:\\;x=1 .\n  \\]\nThe line \\(V\\) is also non\u2011sunny (its slope is infinite).\n\nThus the family  \n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}=\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\cup\\{V\\}\n\\]\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every required point. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*Let \\(n\\ge 3\\). In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at least two of the lines must be non\u2011sunny. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) contains at most one non\u2011sunny line. There are three possible types for a non\u2011sunny line; we treat them separately.\n\n1. **Horizontal non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   Suppose the unique non\u2011sunny line is \\(y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\).  \n   Every required point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq c\\) must belong to a sunny line.  \n   A sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\); therefore it meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point.  \n   The set of required points contains, for each \\(b\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); in particular there are at least \\(n\\) distinct horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) each containing at least one required point not on \\(y=c\\). Consequently we would need at least one distinct sunny line for each such point, i.e. at least \\(n\\) sunny lines. Since \\(|\\mathcal{C}|=n\\), this forces every line to be sunny, contradicting the existence of the non\u2011sunny line \\(y=c\\).\n\n2. **Vertical non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   If the unique non\u2011sunny line is \\(x=c\\), the same argument applied after swapping the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) yields a contradiction.\n\n3. **Diagonal non\u2011sunny line of slope \\(-1\\).**  \n   Suppose the unique non\u2011sunny line is \\(x+y=c\\).  \n   The required points lie on the \\(n\\) distinct diagonals  \n   \\[\n   D_t:\\;x+y=t,\\qquad t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\]\n   The diagonal \\(D_c\\) is already covered by the non\u2011sunny line. For each \\(t\\n