"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and in which exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.*\n\nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) contains the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nI have not been able to prove that no other values of \\(k\\) are possible (the obvious upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) is trivial because there are only \\(n\\) lines). Consequently the answer \u201call integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\u201d is a **partial** solution: the existence part is proved, but the optimality (that no other \\(k\\) can occur) remains unproved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe construction proceeds by induction on \\(n\\).  \nFor a given \\(n\\) and a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines we first place \\(n-k\\) *non\u2011sunny* lines, all taken to be vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\). These lines already cover every lattice point whose \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\).  \n\nThe remaining uncovered points are exactly the points\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\; a+b\\le n+1,\\; a\\ge n-k+1\\},\n\\]\nwhich form a smaller right\u2011angled triangular lattice of size \\(k\\) (its vertices are \\((n-k+1,1),\\,(n,1),\\,(n-k+1,k)\\)).  \n\nThe inductive hypothesis guarantees that this smaller triangle can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct lines, **all of which are sunny** (because the hypothesis is applied to the integer \\(k\\) and we ask for exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines).  \n\nThus we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering the whole original triangle, with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThe base cases \\(n=3\\) and \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are exhibited explicitly, confirming that the induction can be started.\n\nThe construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable. The only obvious restriction is \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines), so the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the natural candidate for the complete answer. Proving that no other values of \\(k\\) can occur would require a separate impossibility argument, which I have not succeeded in providing.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nFor a line \\(L\\) we say that \\(L\\) is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise \\(L\\) is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Inductive covering of a triangular lattice)\n\n*For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_{m}\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWe argue by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=1\\).*  \n\\(T_{1}=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). The single line \\(L_{1}\\) through \\((1,1)\\) with slope \\(2\\) (any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) will do) is sunny and covers the point. Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{1}=\\{L_{1}\\}\\) satisfies the claim.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for some \\(m\\ge 1\\). We construct a covering for \\(m+1\\).\n\nConsider the point \\(P=(1,m+1)\\in T_{m+1}\\). Choose a sunny line \\(L\\) that passes through \\(P\\) and also through the opposite vertex \\(Q=(m+1,1)\\).  \nThe slope of the line \\(PQ\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(m+1)}{(m+1)-1}= \\frac{-m}{m}= -1 .\n\\]\nHence the line \\(PQ\\) is **not** sunny. To obtain a sunny line we perturb the second point slightly: pick the point\n\\[\nQ'=(m+1,2)\n\\]\ninstead of \\(Q\\). The line \\(L\\) through \\(P\\) and \\(Q'\\) has slope\n\\[\n\\frac{2-(m+1)}{(m+1)-1}= \\frac{1-m}{m}= -1+\\frac{1}{m},\n\\]\nwhich is different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\) for every \\(m\\ge 1\\); thus \\(L\\) is sunny.\n\nThe line \\(L\\) contains the points\n\\[\n(1,m+1),\\;(2,m),\\;(3,m-1),\\dots ,\\;(m,2),\\;(m+1,1)\n\\]\nall of which belong to \\(T_{m+1}\\). Removing these \\(m+1\\) points from \\(T_{m+1}\\) leaves exactly the set \\(T_{m}\\) (shifted one unit to the right and one unit upward). By the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) of \\(m\\) sunny lines covering this remaining set.  \n\nConsequently the family\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}:=\\{L\\}\\cup\\mathcal L_{m}\n\\]\nconsists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers all of \\(T_{m+1}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Covering with a prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\n*Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWe treat separately the cases \\(k=n\\) and \\(k<n\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=n\\). The resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{n}\\). Set \\(\\mathcal F_{n,n}:=\\mathcal L_{n}\\).\n\n*Case \\(k<n\\).*  \nPlace \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k .\n\\]\nThese lines are non\u2011sunny and together they cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\nThe points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are **not** covered by the vertical lines are precisely\n\\[\nT'_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nIf we translate the coordinates by the vector \\((-\\,n+k-1,0)\\) (i.e. set \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\)), the set \\(T'_{k}\\) becomes\n\\[\n\\{(a',b)\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}=T_{k}.\n\\]\nThus \\(T'_{k}\\) is a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of size \\(k\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\). Translating each line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) back by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) yields a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers \\(T'_{k}\\).  \n\nFinally define\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n,k}:=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\text{translated lines of }\\mathcal L_{k}\\}.\n\\]\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers the whole set \\(T_{n}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Hence all such \\(k\\) are **admissible**.\n\nThe only obvious restriction is \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines). No further restriction has been proved; consequently the set of admissible values is at least \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). Whether any value outside this set can occur is impossible, because \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\). Thus the natural conjecture is that the admissible values are precisely the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\). The present work establishes the existence part of this conjecture. \u220e"
