"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible (the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) follows because there are only \\(n\\) lines).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Upper bound** \u2013 a line can be either sunny or not, therefore at most \\(n\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny; hence \\(k\\le n\\). This shows that no integer larger than \\(n\\) can occur.\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n\\)** \u2013 we prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there is a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the required lattice points and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Lemma\u202f1* (inductive covering of a triangular lattice) shows that for any \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   can be covered by **exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines**.\n\n   *Lemma\u202f2* uses Lemma\u202f1 to build, for a given \\(n\\) and prescribed \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones:\n   - If \\(k=n\\) we simply take the family supplied by Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=n\\).\n   - If \\(k<n\\) we first place \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\). These cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\). The remaining uncovered points form a smaller triangle \\(T_{k}\\) after a horizontal translation; Lemma\u202f1 provides \\(k\\) sunny lines covering this smaller triangle. Translating those lines back yields the required