            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Notation and definitions  \n\nFor an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the lattice points that must lie on at least one of the lines.\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) lines in the configuration. Each line is either sunny or non\u2011sunny, therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nConsequently no integer larger than \\(n\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 covering a triangle with only sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 1.** *For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal S_{m}\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_{m}\\).*\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n*Base \\(m=1\\).*  \n\\(T_{1}=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). The line  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; y-1 = 2(x-1)\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(2\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{1}=\\{L_{1}\\}\\) works.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for some \\(m\\ge 1\\); we construct a covering for \\(m+1\\).\n\nConsider the two vertices of the triangle \\(T_{m+1}\\),\n\n\\[\nP=(1,m+1),\\qquad Q=(m+1,1).\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny. To obtain a sunny line we move \\(Q\\) one unit upward and set  \n\n\\[\nQ'=(m+1,2).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L\\) through \\(P\\) and \\(Q'\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-(m+1)}{(m+1)-1}= -1+\\frac{1}{m},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for every \\(m\\ge 1\\); thus \\(L\\) is sunny.\n\nA direct computation shows that the integer points of \\(L\\) inside the first quadrant are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(1,m+1),\\;(2,m),\\;(3,m-1),\\dots ,\\;(m,2),\\;(m+1,1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. all points of \\(T_{m+1}\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(m+2\\). Removing these \\(m+1\\) points from \\(T_{m+1}\\) leaves the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich, after translating by \\((-1,0)\\), is precisely \\(T_{m}\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S_{m}\\) of \\(m\\) sunny lines covering this translated copy of \\(T_{m}\\). Translating those lines back by \\((1,0)\\) yields \\(m\\) sunny lines covering the remaining points of \\(T_{m+1}\\).\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{m+1}:=\\{L\\}\\cup\\{\\text{translated lines of }\\mathcal S_{m}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers all of \\(T_{m+1}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 2.** *Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*\n\n- **Case \\(k=n\\).**  \n  Apply Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=n\\). The family \\(\\mathcal S_{n}\\) supplied by Lemma\u202f1 consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{n}\\). Set \\(\\mathcal F_{n,n}:=\\mathcal S_{n}\\).\n\n- **Case \\(k<n\\).**  \n  Place the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{i}:\\; x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n  \\]\n\n  These lines are non\u2011sunny and together they cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n  The points of \\(T_{n}\\) not covered by the vertical lines are  \n\n  \\[\n  T'_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Define a translation \\(\\tau:\\mathbb R^{2}\\to\\mathbb R^{2}\\) by \\(\\tau(x,y)=(x-(n-k),y)\\).  \n  Applying \\(\\tau\\) to \\(T'_{k}\\) yields  \n\n  \\[\n  \\tau(T'_{k})=\\{(a',b)\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}=T_{k}.\n  \\]\n\n  By Lemma\u202f1 there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).  \n  Translating each line of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) back by \\(\\tau^{-1}\\) (i.e. shifting it right by \\(n-k\\) units) gives a family \\(\\mathcal S'_{k}\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers \\(T'_{k}\\).\n\n  Finally set  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_{n,k}:=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal S'_{k}.\n  \\]\n\n  The family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers the whole set \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nThus for every admissible pair \\((n,k)\\) such a configuration exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nCombined with the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) proved in \u00a72.2, we conclude that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any 