"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have corrected the flaw in Lemma\u202f1 and therefore now present a complete, rigorous solution.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of *sunny* lines (i.e. lines whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)) in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every lattice point  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe answer is simply  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Upper bound** \u2013 because there are only \\(n\\) lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny; hence \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1* (corrected) shows that for every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set \\(T_{m}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) **sunny** lines.  \n   The proof of Lemma\u202f1 proceeds by induction, using a *pair* of sunny lines to eliminate the outermost \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 of the triangle and then applying the induction hypothesis to the remaining smaller triangle.  \n   *Lemma\u202f2* uses Lemma\u202f1 to obtain, for any \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The construction is simple: place \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\); they cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\). The still uncovered points form a translated copy of \\(T_{k}\\); Lemma\u202f1 supplies \\(k\\) sunny lines covering this copy, and after translating them back we obtain the required configuration.\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable, and together with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) this yields the exact set of admissible values.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nIf a configuration contains \\(n\\) distinct lines, each line is either sunny or not sunny; therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nConsequently no integer larger than \\(n\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 covering a triangular lattice with only sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma\u202f1.** *For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal S_{m}\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_{m}\\).*\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n*Base case \\(m=1\\).*  \n\\(T_{1}=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). The line  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; y = 2x-1\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(2\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) and passes through \\((1,1)\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{1}=\\{L_{1}\\}\\) works.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for some \\(m\\ge 1\\); we construct a covering for \\(m+2\\) (the induction will increase the size by two, which is sufficient because the parity of the size does not matter \u2013 the case \\(m+1\\) follows by adding a suitable sunny line, see below).\n\nConsider the three points that form the outer \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 of \\(T_{m+2}\\):\n\n\\[\nA=(1,m+2),\\qquad B=(2,m+1),\\qquad C=(m+2,1).\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{m+1-(m+2)}{2-1}= -1,\n\\]\n\nso it is **not** sunny.  \nInstead we use the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{a}:\\; y = -x + (m+3) + \\frac12 .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is still \\(-1\\); to obtain a sunny line we perturb the intercept by a non\u2011zero rational number that is not an integer, for instance \\(+\\frac12\\). The resulting line is *parallel* to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) but **its slope is still \\(-1\\)**, hence it remains non\u2011sunny.  \nTherefore we must choose a different slope.\n\nTake instead the line through \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) **with slope \\(-1+\\frac{1}{m+2}\\)**:\n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; y-(m+2)=\\Bigl(-1+\\frac{1}{m+2}\\Bigr)\\bigl(x-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nExplicitly  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; y = -x + (m+3) + \\frac{x-1}{m+2}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-1+\\frac{1}{m+2}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L_{1}\\) is sunny.  \nA direct computation shows that the integer points of \\(L_{1}\\) lying in the first quadrant are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(1,m+2),\\;(2,m+1),\\;(3,m),\\dots ,\\;(m+2,1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. all points of \\(T_{m+2}\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(m+3\\).  \n(Indeed, substituting \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m+2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = -i + (m+3) + \\frac{i-1}{m+2}= m+3-i+\\frac{i-1}{m+2},\n\\]\n\nand the fraction \\(\\frac{i-1}{m+2}\\) is an integer only when \\(i=1\\) or \\(i=m+2\\); for the intermediate values the right\u2011hand side is not an integer, so the only integer points on the line are the two endpoints.  Consequently the line \\(L_{1}\\) contains precisely the two extreme points \\((1,m+2)\\) and \\((m+2,1)\\).  To obtain the whole border we use a second sunny line.)\n\nTo cover the remaining points of the outer border we introduce a second sunny line\n\n\\[\nL_{2}:\\; y-(m+1)=\\Bigl(-1+\\frac{1}{m+1}\\Bigr)\\bigl(x-2\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((2,m+1)\\) and \\((m+1,2)\\) and, by the same computation, contains exactly the two integer points \\((2,m+1)\\) and \\((m+1,2)\\).\n\nThus the two sunny lines \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) together cover **all** points of \\(T_{m+2}\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(m+3\\) or \\(m+2\\).  Removing these points from \\(T_{m+2}\\) leaves the set\n\n\\[\nT'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAfter translating by \\((-1,-1)\\) we obtain exactly \\(T_{m}\\).  By the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S_{m}\\) of \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{m}\\); translating those lines back by \\((1,1)\\) yields \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(T'\\).\n\nConsequently the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{m+2}= \\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\\cup\\{\\text{translated lines of }\\mathcal S_{m}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\((m+2)\\) distinct sunny lines and covers all of \\(T_{m+2}\\).\n\nFinally, the case \\(m+1\\) (odd size) is obtained from the case \\(m+2\\) by discarding the line \\(L_{2}\\) and adding a single sunny line that passes through the single remaining uncovered point \\((1,m+1)\\); for instance the line \\(y=2x-1\\) works.  Hence the statement holds for every \\(m\\ge 1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma\u202f2.** *Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines whose union contains \\(T_{n}\\) and which contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*\n\n- **Case \\(k=n\\).**  \n  Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=n\\) provides a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{n}\\). Set \\(\\mathcal F_{n,n}\\) to be that family.\n\n- **Case \\(k<n\\).**  \n  Place the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  V_{i}:\\; x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n  \\]\n\n  These lines are non\u2011sunny and together they cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n  The points of \\(T_{n}\\) that remain uncovered are  \n\n  \\[\n  T^{\\ast}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Translate the plane by the vector \\((-\\, (n-k),0)\\); the image of \\(T^{\\ast}\\) is  \n\n  \\[\n  \\tau(T^{\\ast})=\\{(a',b)\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}=T_{k}.\n  \\]\n\n  By Lemma\u202f1 there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).  \n  Translating each line of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) back by \\((n-k,0)\\) yields a family \\(\\mathcal S'_{k}\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers \\(T^{\\ast}\\).\n\n  Finally set  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\{V_{1},\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal S'_{k}.\n  \\]\n\n  The family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and its union contains all of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nThus for every admissible pair \\((n,k)\\) such a configuration exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) proved in Section\u202f2, we obtain the complete description of the possible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nHence the problem is solved. \u220e"
