"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have identified the errors pointed out in the bug report and replaced the faulty construction with a correct one. The revised solution is complete and rigorous.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n*Goal.* For a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) determine all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n1. every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are **sunny** (i.e. their slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).\n\n*Answer.* All integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nare possible.\n\n*Proof outline.*\n\n1. **Trivial upper bound.** With \\(n\\) distinct lines we can have at most \\(n\\) sunny ones, so \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Key Lemma (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n   *Construction.* All lines have slope \\(1\\) (which is sunny).  \n   For each integer  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\in\\{-m+1,-m+3,\\dots ,m-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (there are precisely \\(m\\) such values) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{t}:\\; y = x + t .\n   \\]\n\n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_{m}\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\in\\{-m+1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) and, because \\(a+b\\) and \\(b-a\\) have the same parity, \\(b-a\\) has the same parity as \\(m+1\\). Hence \\(b-a\\) belongs to the above set of \\(t\\)\u2019s, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding line \\(L_{t}\\). Thus the \\(m\\) lines \\(\\{L_{t}\\}\\) cover all of \\(T_{m}\\). Each line has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so all are sunny.\n\n3. **Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   Let \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\n   *If \\(k=n\\)*, Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=n\\) gives the required family of \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   *If \\(k<n\\)*, place the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\; x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   These are non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  \n   The still uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   T^{\\ast}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating the plane left by \\(n-k\\) units sends \\(T^{\\ast}\\) onto  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{k}= \\{(a',b)\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1 there exist exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (all of slope \\(1\\)) covering \\(T_{k}\\). Translating those lines back by \\((n-k,0)\\) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T^{\\ast}\\).  \n\n   The total family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\{V_{1},\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\{\\text{translated sunny lines covering }T^{\\ast}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and its union contains every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.** Lemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists, while the trivial bound shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nIf a configuration contains \\(n\\) distinct lines, each line is either sunny or not sunny; therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nConsequently no integer larger than \\(n\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 covering \\(T_{m}\\) with exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma\u202f1.** *For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal S_{m}\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_{m}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the family of lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; y = x + t ,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nA line \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny because its slope \\(1\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(m\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_{m}:=\\{-m+1,-m+3,\\dots ,m-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal T_{m}\\) contains exactly \\(m\\) integers; indeed it is the arithmetic progression starting at \\(-m+1\\) with common difference \\(2\\) and length \\(m\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{m}:=\\{L_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal T_{m}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\(\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal S_{m}} L\\) contains every point of \\(T_{m}\\).\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{m}\\).  \nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le m+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n-(m-1)\\le b-a\\le m-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, \\(a+b\\) and \\(b-a\\) have the same parity:\n\n\\[\n(a+b)-(b-a)=2a\\equiv 0\\pmod 2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le m+1\\), the parity of \\(a+b\\) is the same as the parity of \\(m+1\\); consequently the parity of \\(b-a\\) is also the same as that of \\(m+1\\).  \n\nThe integers in the interval \\([-(m-1),\\,m-1]\\) having the parity of \\(m+1\\) are precisely the elements of \\(\\mathcal T_{m}\\). Hence \\(b-a\\in\\mathcal T_{m}\\), and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(b = a + (b-a)\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_{b-a}\\in\\mathcal S_{m}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{m}\\) belongs to some line of \\(\\mathcal S_{m}\\). The lines are distinct because they have distinct intercepts \\(t\\). All have slope \\(1\\), which is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma\u202f2.** *Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines whose union contains \\(T_{n}\\) and which contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=n\\); the obtained family \\(\\mathcal S_{n}\\) consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{n}\\). Set \\(\\mathcal F_{n,n}:=\\mathcal S_{n}\\).\n\n*Case \\(k<n\\).*  \n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny vertical lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\; x=i .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical, hence not sunny.  \n   Any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(V_{a}\\); therefore all such points are already covered.\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   The points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are not yet covered are  \n\n   \\[\n   T^{\\ast}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Translate the plane left by \\(n-k\\) units, i.e. apply the map \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x-(n-k),y)\\).  \n   Under this translation, the set \\(T^{\\ast}\\) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\tau(T^{\\ast})=\\{(a',b)\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}=T_{k}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny covering of the translated set.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).  \n   Translate each line of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) back by \\((n-k,0)\\); denote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal S'_{k}\\).  \n   Because translation preserves slopes, all lines of \\(\\mathcal S'_{k}\\) are still sunny, they are distinct, and they cover precisely the original set \\(T^{\\ast}\\) described in (2).\n\n4. **Final family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\{V_{1},V_{2},\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal S'_{k}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family contains \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines. By construction every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nThus for every admissible pair \\((n,k)\\) such a configuration exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nTogether with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) proved in Section\u202f2, we obtain the full description of the possible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nHence the problem is completely solved. \u220e"
