**Lemma\u202f2.** *Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines whose union contains \\(T_{n}\\) and which contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*\n\n*If \\(k=n\\)*, apply Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=n\\); the obtained family \\(\\mathcal S_{n}\\) consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{n}\\). Set \\(\\mathcal F_{n,n}:=\\mathcal S_{n}\\).\n\n*Assume \\(k<n\\).*  \n\n1. **Vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\;x=i .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical, hence not sunny.  \n   Any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(V_{a}\\); therefore all such points are already covered.\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   The points not yet covered are  \n\n   \\[\n   T^{\\ast}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Translate the whole plane left by \\(n-k\\) units, i.e. apply \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x-(n-k),y)\\).  \n   Under this translation, (3) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\tau(T^{\\ast})=\\{(a',b)\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}=T_{k}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny covering of the translated set.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).  \n   Translating each line of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) back by \\((n-k,0)\\) yields a family \\(\\mathcal S'_{k}\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the original set \\(T^{\\ast}\\).\n\n4. **Final family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\{V_{1},V_{2},\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal S'_{k}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family contains \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, all distinct, for a total of \\(n\\) lines. By construction every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nTogether with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) proved in Section\u202f2, we obtain the full description of the admissible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is completely solved. \u220e"
