) has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence all are sunny.\n\n   *Covering argument.*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_{m}\\). Put  \n\n   \\[\n   s:=a+b,\\qquad d:=b-a .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(s\\le m+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le s\\le m+1,\\qquad -(m-1)\\le d\\le m-1. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a=\\frac{s-d}{2}\\) and \\(b=\\frac{s+d}{2}\\) are integers, \\(s\\) and \\(d\\) have the **same parity**:\n\n   \\[\n   s\\equiv d\\pmod 2. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) and (2) two possibilities arise.\n\n   *Case\u202f1.* \\(d\\equiv m+1\\pmod 2\\).  \n   Then \\(d\\) belongs to the set \\(A_{m}\\) (it satisfies the bound \\(-(m-1)\\le d\\le m-1\\) and the required parity).  \n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a+d\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_{d}\\in\\mathcal S_{m}\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f2.* \\(d\\equiv m\\pmod 2\\).  \n   Then \\(d-1\\) has parity \\(m+1\\) and, using (1),  \n\n   \\[\n   -(m-1)\\le d-1\\le m-1,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(d-1\\in A_{m}\\). Moreover \\(d\\) satisfies the bound for \\(B_{m}\\) (because \\(-(m-2)\\le d\\le m-2\\) follows from (1) after removing the two extreme values \\(\\pm(m-1)\\), which correspond precisely to the points where \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\)). Hence \\(d\\in B_{m}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L'_{\\,d-1}: y = x + (d-1)+1 = x+d .\n   \\]\n\n   In both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal S_{m}\\).  \n   Distinctness of the lines follows from the distinctness of their intercepts. \u220e\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (realising any admissible \\(k\\)).**  \n   Let \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n   *If \\(k=n\\)*, Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=n\\) provides a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *If \\(k<n\\)*, place the \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   These lines cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  \n   The still uncovered 